<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flight to Varennes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Flight to Varennes was the attempted escape of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> and their children from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on the night of 20–21 June 1791. Hoping to reach royalist troops near the eastern frontier, they were recognised and stopped at Varennes-en-Argonne and brought back to Paris. The episode destroyed public trust in the king and strengthened republican feeling in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flight-to-varennes.jpg" alt="Flight to Varennes" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Falcon Marshall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrest_of_Louis_XVI_and_his_Family,_Varennes,_1791.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in October 1789 the royal family was moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries Palace</a> and placed under virtual house arrest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> had accepted many actions that disrespected his authority, but his lack of freedom became obvious when the family was prevented from leaving the Tuileries to spend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> at the Château de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cloud">Saint-Cloud</a> in 1791.</p><p>The aim of the escape was to give the king greater freedom of action and personal security. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmedy">Montmédy</a> the marquis de Bouillé had gathered 10,000 regulars of the old Royal Army, including two Swiss and four German regiments, who were thought more reliable in a time of unrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The plan was largely made by the Queen's friend Axel von Fersen the Younger and the baron de Breteuil, who had won the support of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III of Sweden</a>. Fersen urged two light carriages, but that would have split the family, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> had promised never to do, so they chose a single heavy coach drawn by six horses.</p><p>They left the Tuileries at about midnight, in disguise as a Russian baroness and her household. Slow progress, miscalculated times, poor secrecy and a repair to the coach's traces delayed them. Jean-Baptiste Drouet, the postmaster of Sainte-Menehould, recognised the king, perhaps from his portrait on an assignat. Seven cavalry detachments along the route had been withdrawn or neutralised by suspicious crowds, and the family was arrested at Varennes-en-Argonne, 50 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmedy">Montmédy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The crowd in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> met the returning carriage in silence, with men keeping their hats on. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> agreed that the king could remain in power if he accepted the Constitution, but the Cordeliers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> disagreed, and a protest at the Champ de Mars turned violent.</p><p>The king's intentions remain unclear: his Declaration to the French People, left in the Tuileries, accepted civil equality, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a>'s letters sought a return to the old monarchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republicanism">Republicanism</a>, until then a subject of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>-house debate, became the dominant ideal of revolutionary leaders. War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> began in April 1792, the Tuileries was stormed on 10 August 1792, and the First Republic was proclaimed on 21 September. Louis XVI was guillotined on 21 January 1793.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flight to Varennes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The king's attempted flight to <b>Varennes</b> in June 1791 destroyed trust in him, though the Constitution of 1791 kept him as a constitutional monarch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
